class:: KeyMode2
summary:: Tonal key mode
categories:: UGens>Analysis>Tonal
related:: Guides/MIRa, Classes/HPCP, Classes/HarmonicPeaks, Classes/Pitch2

description::
Uses tuning-aware HPCP with exponential time averaging, followed by Pearson correlation against all 24 rotated major/minor profiles. Krumhansl-Kessler data are reported by Temperley (1999); Temperley's 1999 profiles and Faraldo et al.'s EDMA ECIR 2016 measured profiles are also available. The key is the strongest correlation, subject to threshold. This independent implementation uses a twelve-bin HPCP and does not reproduce any external implementation's full preprocessing. Mode 0 means major, 1 minor; root 0 is C through 11 B. Silence or insufficient correlation gives root/mode -1. A local chord, speech or short excerpt may not establish a musical key.

Vanilla link::Classes/KeyTrack:: also estimates tonality. MIRa accepts configurable FFT sizes and tuning, exposes separate root/mode/strength, and uses selectable profiles.

classmethods::
method:: kr
argument:: chain
FFT chain. This analyzer updates when a new frame arrives and holds its output between frames.

argument:: tuning
Reference A in Hz, positive; default 440. This retunes the pitch-class grid.

argument:: profile
One of \krumhansl, \temperley (1999), or \edma (ECIR 2016 measured profiles).

argument:: decay
Exponential key-context averaging time in seconds. Zero gives framewise decisions; default 2 seconds.

argument:: minFreq
Lowest included spectral frequency in Hz.

argument:: maxFreq
Highest included spectral frequency in Hz; zero means Nyquist.

argument:: threshold
Correlation threshold from 0 through 1 for a valid key; default 0.3.

argument:: peakThreshold
Minimum local-peak magnitude relative to the largest included spectral peak, from 0 through 1. Up to 128 strongest peaks are retained.

argument:: fftSize
Literal power of two from 256 through 8192, matching the FFT buffer. Frequency resolution and first-result latency depend on this size.

returns::
Mode index: 0 major, 1 minor, -1 no key.

examples::
KeyMode2 detects the tonal mode of the estimated key (0 for Major, 1 for Minor, -1 for undefined). In this example, detected mode switches the harmony of a synthesized backing triad between bright Major and dark Minor voicings. An interactive code::MIRaGUI.scope:: displays real-time key mode.
code::
(
s.waitForBoot({
    var path = MIRaGUI.soundPath("piano-chords.wav");
    ~miraBuffer = if(path.notNil) { Buffer.read(s, path) } { nil };
    ~miraBus = Bus.control(s, 1);
    s.sync;
    ~miraExample = {
        var source = if(~miraBuffer.notNil) {
            PlayBuf.ar(1, ~miraBuffer, BufRateScale.kr(~miraBuffer), loop: 1)
        } {
            // Synthetic chords fallback
            Mix.ar(SinOsc.ar([261.63, 329.63, 392.00, 523.25] * LFNoise0.kr(0.5).range(1, 1.25))) * 0.15
        };
        // For live input instead: source = SoundIn.ar(0);

        var chain = FFT(LocalBuf(2048), source, 0.25);
        var mode = KeyMode2.kr(chain, decay: 0.3, fftSize: 2048);
        var thirdInt = Select.kr(mode.clip(0, 1), [4, 3]); // 4 for Major, 3 for Minor
        var chordVoice = SinOsc.ar((48 + [0, thirdInt, 7]).midicps, 0, (mode >= 0).lag(0.05) * 0.05).sum;
        var sound = (source * 0.25 ! 2) + (chordVoice ! 2);

        if(~miraBus.notNil) { Out.kr(~miraBus, mode.clip(0, 1)) };
        sound * Line.kr(0, 1, 0.2)
    }.play;

    ~miraGUI = MIRaGUI.scope("Key Mode (0=Maj, 1=Min)", ~miraBus, 0.0, 1.0, "mode");
});
)
::
code::
(
~miraExample.free;
if(~miraGUI.notNil) { ~miraGUI.close };
if(~miraBus.notNil) { ~miraBus.free };
if(~miraBuffer.notNil) { ~miraBuffer.free };
)
::
